FAQ = [
 ("How do I tell a Ram 1500 DT from a Ram 1500 Classic?",
  "Ram sold both from 2019 to 2024. The DT (new body) has a rotary gear selector on the dash on most trims, a larger infotainment screen option and a smoother, more rounded body. The Classic keeps the previous generation's interior with a console or column shifter and a different body. The model-year sticker won't tell you which, but the interior will. Liners are not interchangeable: Husky, for example, sells the 94001 for the DT and the 99001 for the Classic."),
 ("Do Crew Cab liners fit the Quad Cab?",
  "The front row sometimes, the rear never. Husky's 13741 front pair is listed for both Crew Cab and Quad Cab DTs, but the Quad Cab's rear floor is shorter. Every full set on this page is Crew Cab. Quad Cab owners should buy a Quad Cab-specific rear liner."),
 ("What does 'with factory under-seat storage' mean on a Ram listing?",
  "Some Crew Cabs have a storage system under the rear seat cushion, and the rear liner has to be shaped around it. Husky's 94001 and the budget set on this page are listed for trucks with factory under-seat storage. If your rear cushion lifts to reveal bins, you have it. If you see bare carpet, ask the seller whether their rear liner fits a plain floor."),
 ("Are Mopar floor mats better than aftermarket liners?",
  "Mopar mats are factory accessories cut to the truck's floor and sold through Ram dealers. They fit reliably and match the interior. Aftermarket liners like Husky's WeatherBeater usually have taller walls and a published lifetime crack warranty. Choose Mopar if you want the factory look and a dealer part number; choose Husky for containment and warranty."),
 ("Does the rotary shifter make a difference for liners?",
  "Indirectly. Because the DT's shifter is on the dash on most trims, the front center area is open or taken by a large storage console. That makes the center floor visible on bench trucks. Husky sells a separate center hump piece (83211) listed for Crew Cab and Quad Cab DTs if you want that area covered."),
 ("Will these liners fit a 2025 Ram 1500?",
  "The 2025 refresh changed engines and some interior details, but the floor pan carried over. The generic TPE sets here list 2019–2025, while Husky's 94001 title stops at 2024. Confirm 2025 and later with the seller. The TRX and RHO use the same Crew Cab floor, but check the listing for them too."),
 ("How do I keep the driver liner from sliding?",
  "Remove the factory mat, lay the liner on the carpet and lock it onto the retention posts. After fitting, push it forward with your heel; if it moves it isn't seated. Check the brake and accelerator can travel fully without touching the liner edge."),
 ("What's the best way to clean Ram liners?",
  "Take them out, rinse with a hose and scrub with mild soap. Don't use silicone protectants or tire shine, which make the surface slippery. In salt season, dump them every few days so the melt doesn't spill over the walls."),
 ("Do TRX and RHO trucks take the same liners?",
  "The TRX (2021–2024) and the RHO (2025+) are Crew Cab DTs, so they use the Crew Cab floor. Their front seats and console are trim-specific, but the footwell floor is shared. Most listings don't name them, so confirm with the seller, especially for the rear piece if the truck has rear under-seat storage. Husky's 94001 title lists the Crew Cab with factory under-seat storage rather than trims, which is the detail that decides fit."),
 ("Are cheap TPE liners worth it on a Ram?",
  "For many owners, yes. TPE is flexible, easy to clean and holds water in the footwell. What you give up is documentation: budget brands don't publish warranty terms or cold ratings the way Husky does. If the truck is a daily driver in a mild climate, a budget set with the right rear-storage cut is fine."),
]

